---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/sections/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Privacy — Onyx" description="What Onyx does and doesn't collect. It doesn't.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-24">
    <a href="/" class="font-mono text-sm text-accent-primary transition hover:text-accent-glow">
      &larr; Onyx
    </a>
    <h1 class="mt-8 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <p>
        The Onyx app has no analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporting, and no account.
        It makes no network connections of its own except the ones you configure: SSH to
        your hosts, and the APIs you explicitly enable. (This website is a separate
        thing, and does use analytics — see below.)
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything it stores — hosts, favorites, notes, session notes — is a file in
        <span class="font-mono text-text-primary">~/Library/Application Support/Onyx</span>
        on your Mac. Nothing is synced anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Optional integrations talk only to the services you point them at, with
        credentials you supply: GitHub and GitLab for pipelines and pull requests,
        Timing.app for hours, and Apple Reminders through the system framework — which
        macOS gates behind its own permission prompt, and which is read-only.
      </p>
      <p>
        One exception worth stating plainly, because it's the only case where content
        leaves your machine without you pointing it somewhere: the artifact panel renders
        in a web view. Diagrams, models and formatted text load their rendering libraries
        (mermaid, highlight.js, marked) from
        <span class="font-mono text-text-primary">cdn.jsdelivr.net</span>, so displaying
        one makes a request to that CDN. And a <span class="font-mono text-text-primary">PlantUML</span>
        artifact specifically is rendered by
        <span class="font-mono text-text-primary">plantuml.com</span> — the diagram's
        source is encoded into the image URL and sent there. Mermaid diagrams render
        locally in the web view; only the library is fetched. If a diagram's contents are
        sensitive, use mermaid rather than PlantUML.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site — as distinct from the app — is static, served by Cloudflare Pages, and
        loads Google Tag Manager so we can see which pages people actually read. That is
        a third-party script from Google, and depending on the tags configured inside it,
        it may set cookies and record your visit. It runs on this website only: it is not
        in the app, ships in none of the downloads, and can see nothing about your
        machines, sessions or files. Fonts are the only other external request.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-border-subtle bg-bg-secondary/60 p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <span id="analytics-state" class="font-mono text-text-primary">Analytics are on.</span>
          Your choice is remembered in this browser only — there's no account behind it,
          so it doesn't follow you to another device.
        </p>
        <button
          id="analytics-toggle"
          type="button"
          class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-border-subtle px-4 py-2 text-sm text-text-secondary transition hover:border-border-hover hover:text-text-primary"
        >
          Opt out
        </button>
      </div>

      <p class="text-text-muted">
        Questions: <a href="https://github.com/almostrealism/onyx/issues" class="text-accent-primary transition hover:text-accent-glow">open an issue</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var KEY = 'onyx-analytics';
      var label = document.getElementById('analytics-state');
      var button = document.getElementById('analytics-toggle');
      if (!label || !button) return;

      function read() {
        try {
          return localStorage.getItem(KEY);
        } catch (e) {
          return null;
        }
      }

      function render() {
        var off = read() === 'off';
        label.textContent = off ? 'Analytics are off.' : 'Analytics are on.';
        button.textContent = off ? 'Turn analytics back on' : 'Opt out';
      }

      render();

      button.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var next = read() === 'off' ? 'ok' : 'off';
        try {
          localStorage.setItem(KEY, next);
        } catch (e) {
          label.textContent =
            "This browser won't let the site store your choice, so it can't be remembered.";
          return;
        }
        render();
        // Reload either way: off must stop the tag now, and on must
        // actually start it rather than waiting for the next page.
        location.reload();
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Layout>
